---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import Basics from "../../component-examples/tabs/Basics.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="tabs-radio-buttons">
  <section id="the-problem">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Tabs usually mean JavaScript: click handlers, <code>aria-selected</code>,
      hiding and showing panels. CSS-only tabs with radios are old news, but
      they needed the labels grouped apart from the panels and one selector per
      tab to connect them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-trick">
    <h2>The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        One radio group holds the state, and arrow keys move between the radios
        natively
      </li>
      <li>
        The markup interleaves radio, label, panel for each tab, so
        <code>:checked + label + panel</code> shows the right panel without a
        selector per tab
      </li>
      <li>
        A grid puts every label in the first row and the panels in the second,
        and scrolls sideways when the labels don't fit. Tabs that wrap use a
        flex row instead, where <code>order</code> pulls the labels ahead of the
        panels
      </li>
      <li>
        The radios are visually hidden, <code>:focus-visible + label</code>
        draws the focus ring on the label's pill
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>:nth-child(1 of .ui-label)</code> finds the first label among the
        radios and panels, to round the outer corners of the track
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.ui-tabs {
  container-type: inline-size;
  display: grid;
  grid-auto-columns: max-content;
  overflow-inline: auto;
}

.ui-tabs > .ui-label {
  grid-row: 1;
}

.ui-tabs > .ui-panel {
  display: none;
  grid-column: 1 / span calc(sibling-count() / 3);
  grid-row: 2;
  inline-size: 100cqi;
}

.ui-input:checked + .ui-label + .ui-panel {
  display: block;
}

.ui-label:nth-child(1 of .ui-label) {
  border-end-start-radius: var(--ui-radius);
  border-start-start-radius: var(--ui-radius);
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="try-it">
    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>Click a tab, or focus one and use the arrow keys.</p>
    <Example showCode={false}>
      <Basics />
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section id="fallback">
    <h2>Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Browsers without <code>:nth-child(of S)</code> drop those rules: the
        first and last tabs lose their outer rounding, switching still works
      </li>
      <li>
        Browsers without <code>sibling-count()</code> and
        <code>sibling-index()</code> (Firefox before 154, Safari before 26.2)
        drop the declarations that place the radios and span the panels. The
        panel still fills the row, but when the tabs scroll, the arrow keys
        don't scroll the tab they select into view
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>order</code> only moves the tabs visually. Focus follows the DOM,
        which already has the right order: the checked radio, then the content
        of its open panel. No <code>reading-flow</code> needed
      </li>
      <li>
        The rest is <code>:checked</code>, the next-sibling combinator and
        <code>order</code>, supported everywhere
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
